        "4": {
          "evidence": "The state labeled 'UT' is filled with a dark burgundy color, which corresponds to the bottom of the legend scale.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "5": {
          "evidence": "The state in the position of Alabama (labeled 'AL' in the image) is filled with a pale yellow color. The assertion that it is gray and lacks an inscription is incorrect; the gray color is used for other states like California and Arizona, and the AL label is clearly visible.",
          "status": "refuted"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "For this choropleth, a state's fill color encodes incidence according to the vertical legend: the pale-yellow end adjacent to the printed 100 and “High” is higher than the dark-burgundy end adjacent to the printed 0 and “Low.” If the gray Alabama-area polygon denotes unmapped/no incidence value rather than a value on that scale, it is not eligible to exceed a mapped state; among the offered eligible state records, route the highest-incidence one.",
        "evidence": "The necessary condition that the legend governs the fills is supported by the visual evidence (O2). However, the condition regarding the 'gray Alabama-area polygon' is refuted by the visual evidence (O5). Alabama is not gray; it is pale yellow and clearly labeled. Because the premise of the condition (that AL is an unmapped/gray polygon) is false, the logic of the reading (which excludes AL from consideration) is not applicable to the actual chart. The reading relies on a false observation to exclude a state that is actually mapped.",
        "status": "refuted"
      }
    }
  }
}
